# Line-ending policy, and the one place where it is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
#
# Read the fixture section below before changing anything here. This file is not
# housekeeping: without it, `git add` on a machine with core.autocrlf=true silently
# destroys a test fixture in a way that leaves the test passing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LF in the repository and in the working tree, on every platform. Windows tools
# in this stack all accept LF (javac, Maven, PowerShell, modern Notepad), and the
# alternative -- letting each clone decide -- produces review diffs in which every
# line of an untouched file is marked changed, which is how a real one-line change
# gets approved without being read.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SQL: pinned to LF explicitly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /migrations/*.sql are the shipped schema, copied into pip-persistence's resources
# by its pom. MigrationRunner checksums them with CRLF normalised to LF and any BOM
# removed, so the checksum already survives a mixed-platform checkout -- this line
# is defence in depth, not the mechanism. It is here so that a future change to that
# normalisation cannot turn every user's schema_version table into a tamper report.
*.sql text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The fixtures that must NOT be normalised
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# db/fixture-lf and db/fixture-crlf hold the same migration twice: 239 bytes with LF
# and no BOM, 245 bytes with CRLF and a UTF-8 BOM. Their only purpose is to be
# byte-different while normalising to identical text, which is what proves
# MigrationRunnerTest.checksumIgnoresLineEndings actually tests something.
#
# Any normalisation applied to these two paths -- the `* text=auto` above included --
# rewrites one of them so that both end up with the same terminators. The pair then
# differs only by the BOM, and the CRLF half of the assertion silently tests nothing.
# The test still passes. That is the failure mode this rule exists to prevent, and it
# is why the guard assertion in checksumIgnoresLineEndings compares file sizes: if
# these two lines are ever deleted, something fails loudly instead of going quiet.
#
# -text (not `binary`) disables end-of-line conversion while keeping textual diffs,
# which is what you want the day one of these fixtures needs to be read in a review.
#
# `!eol` unspecifies the eol=lf inherited from the `*` line above. Git documents that
# eol has no effect once text is unset, so -text alone is already sufficient -- but
# these are the two files in the repository where relying on an interaction between
# two attributes, rather than stating the intent, is not worth the saved characters.
pip-persistence/src/test/resources/db/fixture-lf/**   -text !eol
pip-persistence/src/test/resources/db/fixture-crlf/** -text !eol

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Scripts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cmd.exe is the one consumer in this repo that genuinely cares. A .cmd file with
# LF-only terminators mostly works, but `goto`/label resolution and a trailing line
# without CRLF are long-standing sources of "works on my machine": force CRLF.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
# Shell scripts must stay LF even in a Windows checkout, or bash reports
# `\r: command not found` on the shebang line.
*.sh   text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Binary: never diffed, never normalised
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sample images live under samples/ and are the inputs to the smoke runs. A single
# CRLF substitution inside a PNG's zlib stream corrupts it, and the error surfaces
# as an ImageIO decode failure far from the cause.
#
# `binary` is a built-in macro for `-diff -merge -text`; the `!eol` is added for the
# same reason as on the fixtures above -- an inherited eol should not even be present
# on a path where a substituted byte is silent corruption.
*.png  binary !eol
*.jpg  binary !eol
*.jpeg binary !eol
*.webp binary !eol
*.bmp  binary !eol
*.gif  binary !eol
*.tif  binary !eol
*.tiff binary !eol
*.ico  binary !eol

# SQLite databases and native artefacts
*.db     binary !eol
*.db-wal binary !eol
*.db-shm binary !eol
*.dll    binary !eol
*.so     binary !eol
*.dylib  binary !eol
*.jar    binary !eol
*.zip    binary !eol
*.exe    binary !eol

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Diff and merge behaviour
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language-aware hunk headers, so a diff says which method changed.
*.java   text diff=java
*.xml    text diff=html
*.html   text diff=html
*.md     text diff=markdown
*.css    text
*.properties text
*.yml    text
*.yaml   text
*.cpp    text diff=cpp
*.h      text diff=cpp
*.cmake  text
CMakeLists.txt text

# Generated reports and lock-like files: exclude from language statistics and
# collapse them in review, where they are noise.
docs/diagrams/*.svg  text linguist-generated=true
*.vmoptions text
